  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Fast westerly flow is bringing in dry air across Vermont \nand northern New York. Temperatures currently range in the upper 60s \nto mid 70s. A well mixed boundary layer has resulted in 20-30 mph \ngusts across the forecast area, and even a few peak gusts around 35-\n40 mph. The expected cold front is still off to our north. Radar \nshows very little activity, and strong 700mb layer inversion with \nlow to mid-level drying will permit only a rogue shower, if any, as \nthe front crosses southeast. Overnight, winds will slacken, but not \ncompletely die off. Additionally, there will be some high clouds \ninitially. This will result in slow cooling brought on by the cold \nadvection early this evening, and then a sharper drop before sunrise \nwhen radiational cooling will become more dominant. Overnight low \ntemperatures should settle into the lower 40s to lower 50s, with \nperhaps a spot upper 30 for the perennially cold Adirondack Airport.\n\nFriday will be nice and cool with beautiful weather with mid 60s to \nlower 70s. Good radiational cooling will ensue overnight, but \nsurface high pressure will amble offshore, and a little bit of \nsoutherly return flow will follow. Another night of lower 40s to \nlower 50s, and probably a few additional upper 30s in colder hollows \nappears most likely. Saturday will be similar, but warming solidly \ninto the 70s with the southerly flow. Then Saturday night will be \nmore humid with warmer overnight lows in the mid 50s to mid 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Sunday will start warm and humid as a narrow tongue of \nGulf moisture reaches the forecast area. There's a fairly strong \nupper low moving along Hudson Bay, but its attendant surface \nreflection will be in far northern Quebec Province. So the cold \nfront extending south of the surface low will be quite elongated \nover our region. A lee side trough enhanced by diurnally driven \nconvection will track along the Mid-Atlantic. It will keep the bulk \nof better instability and moisture away from our region, while we \nonly have marginal synoptic forcing out of the stretched out frontal \nboundary. Still, good surface heating will push temperatures into \nthe mid 70s to lower 80s, and this will result in about 750-1250 \nJ/kg of CAPE. A few showers and thunderstorms seem reasonable, but \nthe GFS simulated IR satellite doesn't hint at the potential for \nstrong updrafts with a lack of cold cloud tops. Shear does appear to \nbe on the high side at 45-50 knots. Given weak forcing, only modest \ninstability, and dry air entrainment, that shear might just be too \nmuch. We'll keep an eye, but the most likely case is garden variety \nscattered showers and thunderstorms with breezy conditions ahead of \nthe front.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: 500 mb flow in the mid-high latitudes are favored to be \nmainly zonal with Arctic air trapped well north of the region. Fast-\nmoving troughs will be moving through this flow with model trends \nshifting to a later approach of Tuesday's wave. As such, chances \nwere lowered Tuesday afternoon and shifted later into Tuesday \nnight/Wednesday as the trough moves through. Some models keep a \nstalled surface boundary over the region which will have \nimplications to temperatures and precipitation chances going \nforward. Should this come to fruition, a wetter period would be \npossible along/south of this boundary. Either way, another wave is \nprojected to move through Thursday into the end of the work week \nwith more chances of afternoon showers and maybe a rumble of \nthunder.",
